From ffc2192456a3937c73b2ab04ee9e8987c16c64db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:03:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] microblaze: Tail-call __syscall_do_cancel

On the cancel path, __syscall_cancel_arch reaches __syscall_do_cancel
with "brlid r15, __syscall_do_cancel", which overwrites r15, the
return address into the cancellable syscall wrapper, and so makes the
hand-written __syscall_cancel_arch a stack frame of its own that
_Unwind_ForcedUnwind must step through.  The assembler in released
binutils cannot assemble .cfi directives for MicroBlaze, so this
function has no .eh_frame entry, and the forced unwind used by
-fexceptions cancellation stops there: cleanup handlers in the caller
never run.  nptl/tst-cancelx4 and nptl/tst-cancelx17 fail for every
cancellation point reached this way.

__syscall_do_cancel is _Noreturn, so the link is not needed.  Branch
to it with "brid" instead, leaving r15 pointing into the syscall
wrapper.  The assembly then contributes no frame:
__syscall_do_cancel, which is C and has unwind information, is
entered as if the wrapper had called it, and the unwind goes from
__syscall_do_cancel to the wrapper to the cleanup handler.

Tested on qemu-system-microblazeel: the early-cancel phase of
nptl/tst-cancelx4 goes from "cleanup handler not called" at every
cancellation point to passing at all of them.  The remaining
in-time and asynchronous failures unwind through a signal frame,
which needs a separate libgcc change.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/syscall_cancel.S | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/syscall_cancel.S b/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/syscall_cancel.S
index d6ae1fcf9c..115ade95e2 100644
--- a/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/syscall_cancel.S
+++ b/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/syscall_cancel.S
@@ -54,7 +54,7 @@ __syscall_cancel_arch_end:
 	nop
 
 1:
-	brlid	r15, __syscall_do_cancel
+	brid	__syscall_do_cancel
 	nop
 
 END (__syscall_cancel_arch)
